#### Open enrollment for 2027 opened Saturday. Benchmark premiums are up another 13%, and 42% in two years. For that money, most of us get a doctor racing a 15-minute clock, and a system that makes “let’s run one more test” the hardest sentence in medicine.

On October 9, I wrote about why you’re [paying more for worse health care](https://v64otd.com/dispatch/you-re-paying-more-for-worse-health-care-everyone-in-charge-knows-it/). Now the final 2027 numbers are out, and the enrollment window is open. You vote tomorrow. Before you do either, here’s what your premium actually buys you inside the exam room, and who in Washington let it get this way.

> **Benchmark premium:** the price of the second-cheapest silver plan in your area. The government uses it to calculate your premium tax credit, so when it rises, the price of coverage rises for nearly everyone shopping in the marketplace.

*Editorial: This Dispatch contains my opinion, clearly marked as such, alongside sourced reporting and my own experience as a patient.*

##### The 2027 Bill

KFF’s open enrollment preview, published October 30, has the final numbers:

- **Benchmark premiums are up 13% on average for 2027,** after a 26% jump for 2026. That’s about 42% in two years.
- **On HealthCare.gov, the average increase is 11%.** In states that run their own marketplaces, it’s 16%.
- **Insurers are leaving.** Eleven carriers have announced they’re exiting at least one state. Cigna is leaving the individual market in all 11 states where it sells.
- **People are dropping coverage.** The Congressional Budget Office projects marketplace enrollment will fall from a peak of 22.4 million in 2025 to 13.1 million in 2027.
- **If you earn more than 400% of the poverty level,** you get no premium tax credit at all. You pay the full price.
- **A new surprise at tax time.** Starting with 2026 tax returns, filed this spring, the caps on how much of an overpaid subsidy you have to pay back are gone. If your income rose during the year, you could owe the IRS the full difference.

Open enrollment runs through December 15 for coverage that starts January 1.

##### What That Money Buys: Fifteen Minutes

Here’s what you get for the bill once you’re in the exam room.

- **The visit is short.** A large study of electronic health records found the average primary care visit lasts about 18 minutes, and many offices book 10- and 15-minute slots that regularly run over. A 2023 follow-up found the doctors with the shortest visits averaged 14 minutes or less per patient. It also found shorter visits linked to more inappropriate antibiotic prescriptions, the kind of shortcut a rushed doctor takes.
- **The math doesn’t work.** In 2022, researchers at the University of Chicago and Johns Hopkins estimated that following national guidelines for an average patient load would require a primary care doctor to work **26.7 hours a day**. “We have an ever-increasing set of guidelines, but clinic slots have not increased proportionately,” the lead author said. For most doctors, he said, the problem is “simply a lack of time.”
- **The paperwork eats the rest.** Doctors handle about 40 prior authorization requests a week, according to the American Medical Association’s latest survey. That’s about 13 hours a week of doctor and staff time spent getting permission from insurers instead of treating patients. Ninety-five percent say it delays care.

I covered the billing model behind this in [Your Doctor Didn’t Choose Fifteen Minutes. Their Employer Did.](https://v64otd.com/dispatch/doctor-didnt-choose-fifteen-minutes/) The short version: doctors are paid for volume. The time it takes to figure out what’s really wrong with you isn’t something anybody pays for.

##### Why Your Doctor Won’t Order the Test

This is the part most people feel and can’t explain. You ask for a test. The doctor says it’s not indicated, or let’s wait and see. Sometimes that’s good medicine. Sometimes it’s the system talking.

**Some insurers won’t pay for tests that break their rules, and neither will you.** Many health plans now hire outside companies to screen lab claims against their own rules. Blue Cross and Blue Shield of Louisiana, for example, began running routine lab claims through an automated program from a company called Avalon in 2022. Its rules limit vitamin B12 testing to once every three months and vitamin D testing to twice a year, and they require that the diagnosis on the order match. If a test doesn’t meet the rules, the insurer’s own notice says, “No payment will be owed,” and the provider “may not bill a member” for it. That protects your wallet. It also means the doctor or lab eats the cost, which gives them a reason to think twice before ordering.

**It works the way it’s designed to.** Avalon says it serves health plans covering more than 21 million people and typically saves them 10% to 18% on outpatient lab spending. By its own numbers, lab orders per member grew 4.9% a year without its claim edits and fell 2.7% a year with them.

**Prior authorization does the same thing for bigger tests.** Imaging and procedures often need insurer approval first. Thirty-two percent of doctors say their requests are often or always denied, and 79% say patients sometimes give up on treatment rather than fight.

**And the clock does the rest.** A test you have to explain, justify, document, and possibly fight for takes time a 15-minute visit doesn’t have.

*Editorial:* None of this means every test should be ordered. Overtesting is real, and some limits make sense; Avalon’s own case study says more than 40% of vitamin D tests it reviewed didn’t meet its rules. But when the system rewards fewer tests, the patients who pay the price are the ones whose problem doesn’t show up on a standard panel, and their problems go unexplained for years. I know, because I was one of them.

##### What It Looks Like From the Patient’s Chair

I’ve been writing about my own case in [My Adrenal Tumors Journey](https://v64otd.com/lifestyle/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-1/), so I’ll keep this short.

For about 15 years, I collected diagnoses: high blood pressure, type 2 diabetes, high cholesterol, sleep apnea, early cataracts. Each one got its own doctor and its own prescription. Starting around 2008 or 2009, I told doctors that I thought something was driving it all. Nobody looked past the symptom in front of them. I had to insist on expanded testing myself.

- **The tests nobody ordered.** My cardiologist was the one who finally said the pattern in my bloodwork looked like something nobody had connected before, and he told me to dig into it myself because he didn’t have time during a cardiology appointment. I found a test that had never been ordered and asked for it. A thyroid antibody test I had to request myself came back elevated. I’d never had a baseline iodine level checked in my life. One test led to another, then to imaging, which found masses on both of my adrenal glands, likely there for years.
- **The 15-minute wall.** At a GI visit this fall, I brought six pages of prepared questions about my iron and B12 not improving. I asked directly for an upper endoscopy. The answer was no, not indicated. At almost exactly fifteen minutes, the PA stood up. The visit was over.
- **What the scope found.** Two days later, the practice called back and recommended the endoscopy after all. The pathology found changes in my stomach lining, including focal intestinal metaplasia, a precancerous change that requires ongoing surveillance. It explained the absorption problems I’d been asking about from the start. I wrote about it in [Chapter 4](https://v64otd.com/lifestyle/my-adrenal-tumors-journey-chapter-4/).
- **The exception.** The best appointment I’ve had in this whole process was an hour with an endocrinologist who took a full history and asked real questions. One hour, in 15 years.
- **Three doctors fired.** This year, I’ve fired three doctors. They weren’t careless or unkind. They just wouldn’t look past the symptom in front of them or dig deeper when the answer didn’t add up. It shouldn’t take three tries to find someone who will, but I’d rather keep looking than wait another fifteen years.

I’m not telling you this for sympathy, and none of it is medical advice. I’m telling you because most of my doctors were capable people doing exactly what the system pays them to do. That’s the problem. And when that isn’t enough for your case, you’re allowed to walk out and find someone who will dig.

##### Who Let It Happen

This is where the Uniparty comes in.

###### **The premium spike:**

- **Democrats wrote the expiration date.** The enhanced premium tax credits were created in 2021 and extended in 2022, on party-line votes, only through the end of 2025.
- **Republicans let them expire.** In January, the House passed a three-year extension by a vote of 230–196, with 17 Republicans joining Democrats. The Republican-led Senate has never voted on it. A bipartisan Senate group has been negotiating a compromise for months. Nothing has passed.

###### **The doctor’s clock:**

- **Medicare doctor pay has fallen by 33% after inflation since 2001**, according to the AMA. Doctors’ Medicare payments are among the few in health care with no automatic yearly inflation adjustment. Both parties have controlled Congress during that time, and neither fixed it.
- **They made it law together.** The 2015 law that set today’s payment system, MACRA, passed with huge bipartisan majorities. Among other things, it set years of 0% base updates for doctors.
- **Another cut is proposed for 2027.** Medicare’s proposed rule for next year would cut the basic payment rate by about 1.7%, mainly because a one-year 2.5% increase Congress passed for 2026 is expiring. When pay per visit falls, practices make up for it with more visits, which means shorter ones.

##### **The paperwork:**

- **The prior authorization bill that passed and vanished.** In 2022, the House passed a bill to rein in Medicare Advantage prior authorization without a single objection. It never became law. This July, two House committees approved it again. It still isn’t law.
- **Congress settled for pledges.** In June 2025, about 60 insurers pledged to streamline prior authorization. In the AMA’s latest survey, only a third of doctors believe the pledge will make a meaningful difference. “Physician trust in voluntary insurer pledges is deeply eroded after years of unfulfilled promises,” AMA President Dr. Bobby Mukkamala said.

*Editorial:* Every piece of this was a choice. Somebody chose a sunset date. Somebody chose not to schedule a vote. Somebody chose to freeze doctors’ pay and then act surprised when visits got shorter. Somebody chose a voluntary pledge over a law. Those somebodies have Rs and Ds after their names, and every two years they ask us to send them back.

##### What This Doesn’t Mean

It doesn’t mean your doctor doesn’t care. Most of mine did. It doesn’t mean every test you want is a test you need, or that every insurer rule is wrong. And it doesn’t mean nothing has changed; some states are filling in for the lost federal credits, and Congress did pass drug-middleman reforms this year.

What it means is that the system pays for speed and penalizes curiosity, and the people with the power to change that keep choosing not to.

##### Call to Action

1. **Shop, don’t auto-renew.** Open enrollment runs through December 15 for January 1 coverage. Your plan may have changed or disappeared. Compare premiums, deductibles, and networks at HealthCare.gov, and check whether your state added its own help.
1. **Plan for tax time.** If your income rises next year, report it to the marketplace right away. The caps on paying back extra subsidy are gone.
1. **Walk in with a list.** Write your questions down, put the most important one first, and ask it in the first two minutes. Leave room for answers, and don’t leave until the top one is answered.
1. **Ask for the test, then ask why not.** If a doctor says no, ask: “What would change your mind?” and “Can you note in my chart that I asked?” If a test is denied, ask for the denial in writing and appeal it.
1. **Get your baselines.** Ask your doctor which baseline numbers make sense for your history so future results have something to compare to.
1. **Fire your doctor if you have to.** If a doctor won’t listen, won’t explain, or won’t dig deeper when your symptoms don’t add up, find another one. Get copies of your records first, so you don’t start from zero. You’re the customer, and in this system you’re also the only one who sees the whole picture.
1. **Use the tools you have.** One of my own doctors told me to use AI to track my health. It helped me organize 15 years of results and turn them into questions. AI isn’t a doctor and can be wrong, so check what it tells you against your doctors' advice. And health information you share with a consumer AI app generally isn’t protected by HIPAA, the federal medical privacy law.
1. **Vote tomorrow, and ask one question first.** Ask every name on your ballot what they’ve actually done about premiums, prior authorization, and doctors’ pay. If the answer is a talking point about the other party, or even yours, you have your answer.

You’re paying more every year. What you’re getting is a doctor watching the clock and a test that never got ordered. The people who could fix it are on your ballot tomorrow, or their party is.

V64OTD // MORE PREMIUM. LESS DOCTOR.

